@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Dorks & Dice: Leadership, Systems Design, and Technical Operations";
    var header = new dorks_and_dice_site.Models.Resume.ResumeDetailHeaderViewModel
    {
        Title = "Dorks & Dice: Leadership, Systems Design, and Technical Operations",
        Subtitle = "A long-term project combining facilitation, research, infrastructure, and software planning",
        MetaLine = "Founder | September 2024 - Present | Game Master through April 2026 | Jacksonville, FL",
        InfoItems = new Dictionary<string, string>
        {
            ["Role"] = "Founder, Game Master, systems designer, and technical operator",
            ["Workload"] = "Up to 12-hour sessions with 24+ hours of weekly preparation",
            ["Core strengths"] = "Requirements analysis, facilitation, systems thinking, and project ownership",
            ["Technical scope"] = "Web development, Linux servers, networking, containers, and deployment",
            ["Current focus"] = "Initiative and complex encounter tracking"
        }
    };
}

@await Html.PartialAsync("~/Views/SiteModes/Professional/Resume/_ResumeDetailHeader.cshtml", header)

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Overview</h2>
<p>
    Dorks &amp; Dice is a long-term project through which I developed practical experience in leadership,
    requirements analysis, systems design, research, documentation, technical operations, and software planning.
    I founded the group in September 2024, ran its principal Dungeons &amp; Dragons campaign through April 2026,
    and built the processes and infrastructure that supported the group beyond individual sessions.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Planning And Facilitation</h2>
<p>
    Sessions lasted up to 12 hours, with at least two dozen additional hours of weekly preparation. I maintained
    continuity across interconnected scenarios, characters, factions, rules, and long-term consequences while adapting
    in real time to decisions I could not predict in advance.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Combined long-range planning with weekly execution</li>
    <li>Made rapid decisions under incomplete and changing information</li>
    <li>Facilitated extended collaborative sessions and communicated decisions clearly</li>
    <li>Managed scheduling, expectations, and significant interpersonal conflict</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Participant-Centered Systems Design</h2>
<p>
    I treated player goals as requirements to understand rather than requests to reject because they fell outside a
    prepared script. For almost anything the players wanted to attempt, I worked with them to determine how it could
    function, what constraints applied, and what consequences would follow.
</p>
<p>
    This required translating loosely defined ideas into workable systems, supporting multiple valid approaches,
    and balancing user freedom with consistency, fairness, and maintainability. The same skills apply directly to
    client discovery, requirements gathering, and user-centered product design.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Research And Adaptation</h2>
<p>
    Supporting player-driven ideas regularly required rapid domain learning. For example, I studied basic blacksmithing
    and metallurgy to better support a player focused on crafting and item invention. I applied the same process across
    vehicle systems, large-scale encounters, and interstellar travel.
</p>
<p>
    The campaign ultimately expanded far beyond its original scope without losing continuity. Its finale involved several
    simultaneous objectives across ground, aerial, and strategic conflicts and remained challenging for hours despite a
    final attack dealing 1,372 damage. That outcome required managing dependencies, parallel states, and long-term consequences
    rather than relying on a single conventional encounter structure.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Process Improvement And Software Planning</h2>
<p>
    Complex boss encounters exposed limitations in conventional initiative trackers. I adapted a multi-part boss framework
    using separate weak points, rage, encounter mechanics, and evolving states, but the manual tracking burden remained high.
</p>
<p>
    My first planned Dorks &amp; Dice web tool is an initiative and encounter tracker designed around that workflow. It will
    manage standard initiative alongside weak-point health, rage, mechanics, and boss state. The project follows a practical
    product-development process: identify a recurring problem, determine why existing tools fail, define the required behavior,
    and build a reusable solution around real use.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Technical Operations</h2>
<p>
    Dorks &amp; Dice also expanded into persistent services. I launched and operated Minecraft and Hytale servers supported by
    the group's website and Discord presence. This work required hands-on experience with hardware, Linux, containers,
    networking, remote access, deployment, troubleshooting, and ongoing maintenance.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Professional Skills Demonstrated</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Requirements analysis and user-centered design</li>
    <li>Systems thinking and dependency management</li>
    <li>Long-term project ownership and execution</li>
    <li>Facilitation, leadership, and conflict resolution</li>
    <li>Research and rapid domain learning</li>
    <li>Decision-making under changing constraints</li>
    <li>Documentation and continuity management</li>
    <li>Linux server, networking, container, and deployment experience</li>
    <li>Converting repeated workflow problems into software requirements</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Current Direction</h2>
<p>
    The major tabletop campaigns are complete, while the Minecraft server, Hytale server, website, and Dorks &amp; Dice identity
    provide a foundation for future work. My immediate development focus is the initiative and complex encounter tracker,
    followed by additional Game Master tools derived from workflows I experienced directly.
</p>

